WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:01.778
This is WriteSight.

2
00:00:01.778 --> 00:00:05.921
A Microsoft Word add-in that records
how a document was written, and gives

3
00:00:05.921 --> 00:00:08.191
faculty a readable view of that process.

4
00:00:08.691 --> 00:00:11.066
For the student there
is almost nothing to it.

5
00:00:11.066 --> 00:00:14.285
They open the assignment, enter
the code their instructor gave

6
00:00:14.285 --> 00:00:15.974
them, and press Begin recording.

7
00:00:16.424 --> 00:00:19.531
From then on it runs whenever
the document is open.

8
00:00:19.531 --> 00:00:21.644
Nothing to start, nothing to stop.

9
00:00:22.094 --> 00:00:26.550
What it records is the shape of the work.
Typing, pausing, pasting, revising.

10
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.477
It never records the writing itself,
and the record stays inside the

11
00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:32.412
document rather than on a server.

12
00:00:32.912 --> 00:00:37.088
When the instructor opens that same
document, they see something different.

13
00:00:37.088 --> 00:00:37.596
A report.

14
00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:39.533
It opens with a sentence.

15
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:43.467
This document was mostly typed in
place, built in three sessions.

16
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:45.056
Then one of two boxes.

17
00:00:45.056 --> 00:00:48.629
Either nothing about how the document
was built stands out, or here is

18
00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:50.389
what might be worth a closer look.

19
00:00:50.839 --> 00:00:53.591
Each observation is one plain sentence.

20
00:00:53.591 --> 00:00:57.755
About 320 words were already
present before recording began.

21
00:00:57.755 --> 00:01:01.142
Notice what it does not say.
It does not say why.

22
00:01:01.642 --> 00:01:03.078
Below that, the figures.

23
00:01:03.078 --> 00:01:07.026
How much was typed, how much was
pasted, how many sittings, and how

24
00:01:07.026 --> 00:01:09.180
long the writer was actually active.

25
00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.303
Then the timeline, which is the
part faculty look at longest.

26
00:01:13.303 --> 00:01:15.506
A steady climb is someone composing.

27
00:01:15.506 --> 00:01:18.934
A vertical cliff is a block
of text arriving all at once.

28
00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:21.286
The record also checks itself.

29
00:01:21.286 --> 00:01:24.497
If it was altered after the
fact, the report says so.

30
00:01:24.947 --> 00:01:30.051
What you will not find anywhere is a score.
No percentage, no risk level, no accusation.

31
00:01:30.051 --> 00:01:33.865
WriteSight produces no verdict,
because a verdict is the one thing

32
00:01:33.865 --> 00:01:35.918
a tool cannot responsibly hand you.

33
00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:39.832
WriteSight is in pilot, and what
it becomes next is being worked

34
00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:41.434
out with the faculty using it.

35
00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:46.234
A view for the writer, so a student can
show their process rather than defend it.

36
00:01:46.234 --> 00:01:48.984
A sense of how one writer's
work changes over time.

37
00:01:48.984 --> 00:01:52.009
Patterns at the level of a
program rather than a person.

38
00:01:52.509 --> 00:01:54.850
And one we have deliberately not built.

39
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:59.171
InForm, our other add-in, checks
whether the sources in a paper are real.

40
00:01:59.171 --> 00:02:03.252
Putting that inside this report is
a small step, but a process record

41
00:02:03.252 --> 00:02:07.393
beside an unverified source invites
a conclusion neither one supports.

42
00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:08.773
That gets solved first.

43
00:02:09.323 --> 00:02:13.386
WriteSight, from ScholarVera.
In pilot with universities now.

44
00:02:13.386 --> 00:02:17.178
Tell us about your program at
scholarvera.com/writesight.
